   "label": "Tricolon",
   "quote": "sive perfidia ... sive invidia ... sive quod ... gestiebat",
   "trans": "whether through treachery ... or envy ... or because it longed ...",
   "body": "The narrator offers three guesses for why the lamp spilled its oil, in a rising tricolon of <em>sive</em> clauses: wicked treachery, harmful envy, or simple desire to touch the god. The mock-uncertainty teases the reader and personifies the lamp as a jealous accomplice.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The tricolon <em>sive ... sive ... sive</em> playfully speculates on the lamp’s motive, personifying it as treacherous, envious or love-struck — a witty digression that delays and heightens the catastrophe.\"",
   "line": ""
